window.AU = window.AU || {};
window.AU.exams = window.AU.exams || {};
window.AU.exams["associate-foundations"] = {"examId":"associate-foundations","title":"Claude Certified Associate – Foundations","shortTitle":"Associate – Foundations","track":"Associate","level":"Foundations","unofficial":true,"meta":{"questionCount":100,"passScaled":720,"scaleMin":100,"scaleMax":1000,"timeMinutes":120},"guide":"# Claude Certified Associate – Foundations — Study Guide\n\n> **Unofficial, community-authored study material.** Not affiliated with, endorsed by, or produced by Anthropic. It teaches the publicly documented concepts the exam covers; the questions here are original and are **not** real exam items.\n\nThe Associate – Foundations exam is about using Claude well as a knowledge worker: choosing the right place to work, writing prompts that get usable results, judging whether an output can be trusted, and folding all of that into a team's workflow safely. You are not expected to write code or design production systems. You *are* expected to make sound, practical decisions — and to know when a human, not the model, must have the final say.\n\nA useful mental model runs through every domain: **set yourself up (entry point, model, features, context) → prompt clearly → evaluate the result → integrate it responsibly.** The choices you make *before* prompting set the quality ceiling; the checks you make *after* keep you out of trouble.\n\nThis guide follows the seven exam domains. For each, it explains the key concepts, the decision rules, and the traps the exam likes to test.\n\n## Platform & Model Foundations\n\nClaude meets you through several **entry points**, and picking the right one is the first decision:\n\n| Entry point | Best for |\n| --- | --- |\n| **claude.ai chat** | Quick, one-off, conversational tasks with no reusable setup |\n| **Projects** | Repeated work that reuses the same instructions and reference material |\n| **Claude Code** | Reading, editing, and running code across a local repository |\n| **API** | Building Claude into your own software to run programmatically |\n\nThen choose a **model** to match the task:\n\n- **Claude Opus 4.8** — the most capable model; reserve it for the hardest, multi-step reasoning where accuracy outweighs cost and speed.\n- **Claude Sonnet 5** — the balanced default for high-volume, moderately complex work.\n- **Claude Haiku 4.5** — fastest and most economical for simple, high-volume tasks like classification.\n\nThe classic trap is running *everything* on the largest model \"to be safe.\" That wastes cost and latency; **match the model to the task** instead.\n\nKnow the **features** and what each is actually for: **extended (adaptive) thinking** improves multi-step reasoning; **web search** retrieves information newer than the training cutoff; **artifacts** give you an editable side panel for substantial content; **file uploads** let Claude read a real document (like a PDF) instead of a paraphrase. A common distractor swaps these — e.g., offering \"extended thinking\" for a question that needs *current facts* (that needs web search) or \"artifacts\" for a *reasoning* problem.\n\nFinally, **manage context within a session.** Everything earlier in a conversation is context Claude may draw on, so when a long chat drifts across topics and answers get noisy, start a fresh conversation. Give Claude the *relevant* material and a specific question rather than dumping hundreds of irrelevant pages.\n\n## Prompting & Task Execution\n\nGood prompts are **structured**: a clear **role**, the **task**, relevant **context**, the desired **output format**, and **examples** where helpful. Missing pieces show up as predictable failures — a generic draft usually means a generic prompt.\n\nCore habits the exam rewards:\n\n- **Be specific.** Name the exact scope, format, and success criteria. Vague asks like \"make this better\" fail because \"better\" is undefined — say *clearer structure*, *shorter*, *more data-driven*.\n- **Decompose complex requests.** If one prompt asks for three things and each comes out shallow, break the work into focused steps and check each before it feeds the next.\n- **Show, don't just tell.** When a specific format or transformation keeps coming out wrong, a couple of worked **input → output examples** (few-shot) communicate the pattern better than more prose. Reserve them for tasks with a particular format or tricky edge cases; simple tasks rarely need them.\n- **Say what to do, not only what to avoid.** \"Don't be so formal\" underperforms \"warm and conversational, like advice to a colleague, with a short example.\"\n- **Iterate diagnostically.** Change **one thing at a time** and observe. If you rewrite the role, add examples, change the format, and swap models all at once, you can't tell which change helped.\n\n> Remember: Claude only knows what you give it. For anything about your private policies, products, or data, you must **provide that context** — a bigger model has still never seen your internal documents.\n\n## Evaluating & Validating Output\n\nClaude can be **fluently, confidently wrong**. Tone and polish say nothing about accuracy, so treat confidence as a non-signal.\n\nWhat to check:\n\n- **Facts and citations.** Verify claims against a primary source. Claude can **hallucinate** authoritative-looking citations that don't exist, and a *real, working* link still needs checking that the source actually says what's attributed to it.\n- **Suspicious precision.** Oddly exact figures with no source (e.g., \"$4.37B in 2023\") are a classic fabrication pattern — precision is not provenance.\n- **Bias.** Skewed outputs, especially in decisions about people, are a signal to examine the criteria and keep human oversight.\n\nThe central rule is **verification scales with stakes and reversibility.** Low-stakes, reversible, subjective work (brainstorming names) needs little scrutiny. **High-stakes, factual, legal/medical, or irreversible** outputs demand independent human verification — a lawyer for a binding contract, a clinician for a dosage, a careful review before a permanent deletion or a figure you submit to the authorities. And when you send Claude's work **under your own name, you are accountable for it.** A long track record of good results doesn't make outputs infallible; dropping review entirely just lets the inevitable errors through.\n\n## Workflow Integration & Solution Design\n\nThe recurring question is **what to delegate versus keep with a human.** Delegate the **well-specified, repeatable, checkable, low-risk** steps (reformatting, first drafts, templated messages). Keep the **ambiguous, high-stakes, accountability-bearing** judgments (personnel decisions, who advances in hiring, signing off on official figures). The deciding factors for whether an action can run automatically are **how reversible it is and how costly a mistake would be** — cheap and reversible can auto-proceed; costly or irreversible warrants human approval, placed exactly where the stakes are.\n\nAccountability always rests with a **named human owner**; a tool cannot be answerable for a decision.\n\nWhen **communicating to stakeholders**, be honest about both value *and* limits. Overpromising (\"fully autonomous, error-free, replaces the team\") destroys trust at the first mistake. Credible value framing pairs the real benefit (faster first drafts, shorter turnaround) with the human role that preserves quality, and backs it with concrete before/after measures rather than a single anecdote.\n\nTo move from **\"I use Claude\" to \"our workflow uses Claude,\"** turn personal know-how into shared, reusable, documented assets — prompts, Project instructions, and knowledge — so results are consistent and the workflow survives your absence. And match the **investment to the need**: don't build an elaborate system for a task done by hand twice a year.\n\n## Configuration & Knowledge Management\n\n**Claude Projects** are the Associate's main configuration tool. Two building blocks:\n\n- **Custom instructions** — standing behavioral rules (role, tone, structure) that apply **automatically to every chat** in the Project.\n- **Knowledge sources** — reference documents Claude can draw on across every chat.\n\nMatch each to its slot: reference material goes in **knowledge sources**, behavioral rules go in **custom instructions**. The payoff is leverage — invest in good context once, and **every** conversation in the Project inherits it, raising quality and consistency across the board. That also means durable, always-apply rules belong in the Project, while a one-off request belongs in a single prompt.\n\nThe dominant failure mode here is **stale configuration.** If knowledge holds last year's prices or an old policy, Claude will confidently repeat it. The **root-cause fix is to update the source**, not to correct each chat by hand. Keep configuration current on a regular cadence, remove **conflicting or outdated** versions so Claude can't cite the wrong one, and scope Projects tightly (separate Projects for unrelated kinds of work). When teams drift because everyone keeps a private copy of the instructions, consolidate into **one shared Project as the source of truth**. Vague instructions (\"be professional\") produce varied output; specify structure, length, and tone.\n\n## Governance, Risk & Responsible Use\n\nResponsible use starts with **data awareness: know where your input goes, how it may be used or retained, and whether policy permits it** — *before* you paste anything.\n\nFirm rules:\n\n- **Never paste secrets** (passwords, live API keys, full card numbers). Asking Claude to \"ignore\" a key doesn't undo the exposure.\n- **Regulated personal data** (PII/PHI) requires following your data policy and getting authorization first; identifiable patient records raise health-privacy obligations.\n- **Confidential data belongs only in approved tools.** Deleting the chat afterward doesn't retroactively make an unapproved destination compliant.\n- **Practice data minimization** — include only what the task needs; anonymize or strip details you don't. Review outputs before sharing externally so nothing sensitive leaks.\n\nUnderstand **data classification** (Public / Internal / Confidential / Restricted): the label sets handling rules, and some classes may not be entered into certain tools at all. Beware **shadow IT** — personal, unvetted accounts route company data outside sanctioned controls. And **responsible-use judgment** means declining requests that facilitate harm or unauthorized access, regardless of how they're framed. The soundest org rule *enables safe use*: approved tools, follow the classification policy, no secrets or regulated data without authorization — not a blanket ban.\n\n## Troubleshooting & Optimization\n\nTroubleshooting is **root-cause diagnosis, then a durable fix.** When an output underperforms, ask which of a few usual suspects is to blame:\n\n- **Generic answers →** missing context; add the specifics of your situation.\n- **Format keeps coming out wrong →** stop re-describing it and give a concrete example.\n- **Simple task slow/expensive on a big model →** wrong model; move to a smaller, faster one. **Complex task too shallow on a small model →** move up (and enable extended thinking).\n- **Confidently out of date →** enable web search; the gap is the training cutoff, not reasoning.\n- **Painful file-by-file pasting of a codebase →** wrong entry point; use Claude Code.\n- **Ambiguous instruction (\"clean up this\") →** specify exactly what to change.\n\nTwo meta-skills: **isolate variables** (if an edit made things worse, revert and reintroduce changes one at a time), and **fix durably, not once.** A correction you repeat every day, or a tweak that reliably helps the whole team, belongs in the **shared Project configuration** so every future chat benefits automatically — not in a one-off patch you have to remember to apply.\n","domains":[{"id":"platform-models","name":"Platform & Model Foundations","weight":0.15},{"id":"prompting","name":"Prompting & Task Execution","weight":0.15},{"id":"evaluating","name":"Evaluating & Validating Output","weight":0.15},{"id":"workflow","name":"Workflow Integration & Solution Design","weight":0.14},{"id":"configuration","name":"Configuration & Knowledge Management","weight":0.14},{"id":"governance","name":"Governance, Risk & Responsible Use","weight":0.14},{"id":"troubleshooting","name":"Troubleshooting & Optimization","weight":0.13}],"questions":[{"id":"as-001","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Entry Points","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"A developer wants Claude to read, edit, and run tests across the files of a local Git repository from the terminal. Which entry point is the best fit?","options":{"A":"claude.ai chat, pasting the files in one at a time","B":"Claude Code, which works directly with your repository in the terminal","C":"A Claude Project with the repository zipped as a knowledge source","D":"The API with a custom script the developer writes and maintains"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Claude Code is the terminal-based entry point built to read, edit, and run code across a repository directly. It is purpose-built for hands-on local codebase work.","explanationDistractor":"A Project knowledge source is read-only reference material; it cannot edit files or run tests, so zipping the repo into a Project does not enable the work described.","reference":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview"},{"id":"as-002","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Entry Points","scenario":"Customer Support Team","difficulty":"easy","stem":"Your team answers customer questions using the same product FAQ, tone guidelines, and policy documents every day, and you want every chat to start with that context without pasting it each time. Which entry point best fits?","options":{"A":"A new claude.ai chat each time, pasting the documents at the start","B":"Claude Code, with the documents committed to a repository","C":"A Claude Project with the FAQ and policies as knowledge and the tone in custom instructions","D":"The API, embedding all the documents in every single request"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Projects let you set durable custom instructions and attach knowledge sources, so every chat in the Project inherits that context automatically. That is ideal for a repeated workflow with shared reference material.","explanationDistractor":"Re-pasting the documents into a fresh chat each time is exactly the manual repetition Projects eliminate; it is error-prone and easy to forget.","reference":null},{"id":"as-003","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Entry Points","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"An engineer needs Claude to classify incoming support tickets automatically inside the company's own application, with no person in the loop. Which entry point is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"The Claude Developer Platform API, called from the application's code","B":"claude.ai chat, with a staff member pasting each ticket manually","C":"A Claude Project shared with the whole support team","D":"Claude Code running in a developer's terminal"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Programmatic, automated classification inside your own software is what the API is for: the application sends each ticket to Claude and acts on the response with no manual steps.","explanationDistractor":"claude.ai chat requires a person to paste each ticket and read each answer, which defeats the goal of automatic, in-app classification.","reference":null},{"id":"as-004","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Entry Points","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"A marketer wants to brainstorm ten taglines for a campaign and refine the best few in a quick back-and-forth. There is no code, no repeated workflow, and nothing confidential. Which entry point is the simplest appropriate choice?","options":{"A":"The API, driven by a Python script","B":"Claude Code in the terminal","C":"A dedicated Claude Project with several knowledge sources","D":"A claude.ai chat conversation"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"For a quick, one-off, conversational task with no code or reusable context, a plain claude.ai chat is the simplest fit; no extra setup is warranted.","explanationDistractor":"Creating a Project with knowledge sources is unnecessary overhead for a single throwaway brainstorm that will not be repeated.","reference":null},{"id":"as-005","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Model Selection","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"You need Claude to work through a complex, multi-step financial analysis that requires careful reasoning, and where subtle mistakes are costly. Speed and cost are secondary. Which model is the best default?","options":{"A":"Claude Haiku 4.5, for the fastest possible response","B":"Claude Opus 4.8, for the strongest reasoning","C":"Whichever model is cheapest per token that day","D":"Claude Sonnet 5, chosen mainly to minimize latency"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Opus 4.8 is the most capable model for the hardest, multi-step reasoning. When accuracy on a complex task outweighs speed and cost, it is the right default.","explanationDistractor":"Haiku 4.5 is optimized for speed and simple tasks; using it for costly, subtle multi-step reasoning trades away the capability the task demands.","reference":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview"},{"id":"as-006","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Model Selection","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A product feature will summarize thousands of documents per day. Each summary is moderately complex, and you need a strong balance of quality, speed, and cost at scale. Which model is the most appropriate default?","options":{"A":"Claude Opus 4.8 for every request, regardless of cost","B":"Claude Haiku 4.5, accepting weaker quality on the harder documents","C":"Claude Sonnet 5, balancing quality and cost at volume","D":"A model chosen at random per request to average the cost"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Sonnet 5 is designed for the balanced, high-volume middle ground: strong quality at a cost and speed that scale to thousands of requests. It fits moderately complex work at volume.","explanationDistractor":"Running Opus 4.8 on every request maximizes quality, but its cost and latency are hard to justify across thousands of moderately complex summaries per day.","reference":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview"},{"id":"as-007","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Model Selection","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"You need to detect whether each incoming message is written in English or Spanish: a simple, high-volume, latency-sensitive classification. Which model is the best fit?","options":{"A":"Claude Haiku 4.5, the fastest and most economical option","B":"Claude Opus 4.8, to be safe on accuracy","C":"Whichever model has the largest context window","D":"A separate Opus call per language, then compare them"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Haiku 4.5 is built for simple, fast, high-volume tasks like language detection, delivering low latency and low cost where heavier reasoning is not needed.","explanationDistractor":"Reaching for Opus 4.8 \"to be safe\" on a trivial classification wastes cost and latency without meaningfully improving a task Haiku handles well.","reference":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview"},{"id":"as-008","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Features","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A logic puzzle keeps tripping Claude up because it involves several dependent reasoning steps. Which built-in capability is most directly aimed at improving multi-step reasoning like this?","options":{"A":"Web search, to look the answer up online","B":"Extended (adaptive) thinking, which lets Claude reason through steps before answering","C":"File uploads, to attach the puzzle as a document","D":"Artifacts, to render the answer in a side panel"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Extended (adaptive) thinking gives Claude room to reason through intermediate steps before committing to an answer, which directly helps on multi-step problems.","explanationDistractor":"Artifacts only change how output is displayed in a side panel; they do nothing to improve the underlying reasoning on a multi-step problem.","reference":null},{"id":"as-009","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Features","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"You ask Claude about a regulation that changed last week, after the model's training cutoff. Which feature should you enable to get an accurate, current answer?","options":{"A":"Extended thinking, to reason harder about it","B":"Artifacts, to format the answer nicely","C":"Web search, so Claude can retrieve up-to-date information","D":"A larger maximum output length"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Web search lets Claude retrieve current information from the internet, which is essential for facts that changed after its training cutoff. Reasoning alone cannot recover knowledge the model never had.","explanationDistractor":"Extended thinking helps Claude reason about what it already knows; it cannot conjure facts about an event that postdates its training data.","reference":null},{"id":"as-010","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Features","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You are drafting a policy document with Claude and want to see it in a dedicated panel, edit it across turns, and keep a stable version as you refine it. Which feature is designed for this?","options":{"A":"Artifacts, a side panel for substantial, editable content","B":"Web search, to gather sources for the document","C":"Extended thinking, to plan the document","D":"Token counting, to measure the document's size"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Artifacts render substantial, self-contained content (like a document or code) in a dedicated, editable side panel you can iterate on across turns, which is exactly this use case.","explanationDistractor":"Web search retrieves information; it has nothing to do with displaying and iterating on a document you are co-writing.","reference":null},{"id":"as-011","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Features","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"A colleague sends you a 40-page PDF contract and asks for a plain-language summary of its key obligations. In claude.ai, what is the most direct way to have Claude work from the actual document?","options":{"A":"Retype the contract into the chat box by hand","B":"Upload the PDF file directly to the conversation","C":"Describe the contract from memory and ask Claude to guess the rest","D":"Ask Claude to search the web for the contract"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"claude.ai supports file uploads, including PDFs, so Claude can read the actual document and summarize it accurately rather than working from a paraphrase.","explanationDistractor":"Describing the contract from memory strips out the exact terms Claude needs and invites an inaccurate summary of a document Claude never actually saw.","reference":null},{"id":"as-012","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Context Management","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"After a long chat that drifted across several unrelated topics, Claude's answers start pulling in irrelevant details from earlier in the conversation. What is the best way to manage this?","options":{"A":"Keep going and hope the noise clears on its own","B":"Repeat your question in all capital letters","C":"Start a new conversation focused only on the current topic","D":"Switch to a smaller, faster model"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Everything earlier in a conversation is context Claude may draw on. When accumulated, unrelated history starts to interfere, a fresh conversation gives Claude a clean, focused context.","explanationDistractor":"Switching to a smaller model does not remove the irrelevant conversation history that is actually causing the interference.","reference":null},{"id":"as-013","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Context Management","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You want Claude to answer a specific question about one section of a large report. Which approach gives Claude the most useful context to work with?","options":{"A":"Paste the entire multi-hundred-page report and ask the question at the very end with no guidance","B":"Provide the relevant section (or a focused excerpt) and state the specific question","C":"Ask the question with no context and let Claude infer the report","D":"Send the question first, then the report in a separate message hours later"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Giving Claude the relevant section plus a clear, specific question focuses its attention on what matters. Focused, on-point context generally produces better answers than dumping everything or nothing.","explanationDistractor":"Dumping hundreds of irrelevant pages buries the pertinent section and can dilute Claude's attention, making a precise answer harder rather than easier.","reference":null},{"id":"as-014","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Entry Points","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You find yourself pasting the same brand style guide into a new chat several times a week to keep Claude's writing on-brand. What is the better long-term setup?","options":{"A":"Keep pasting it each time; it only takes a minute","B":"Memorize the guide so you can paraphrase it faster","C":"Create a Project with the style guide as knowledge and the brand voice in custom instructions","D":"Email the style guide to Claude and ask it to remember"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"When the same reference is reused repeatedly, moving it into a Project (knowledge source plus custom instructions) means every chat inherits it automatically: durable, consistent, and no re-pasting.","explanationDistractor":"Continuing to paste the guide every time is the manual, error-prone repetition that a Project is designed to eliminate.","reference":null},{"id":"as-015","domain":"platform-models","studyArea":"Model Selection","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A teammate runs every task, even trivial reformatting, on the largest, most expensive model \"to be safe.\" What is the most sensible guidance?","options":{"A":"Always default to the largest model, since extra reasoning capability is never actually wasted","B":"Match the model to the task, using a smaller model for simple work and the largest for hard reasoning","C":"Always default to the smallest model available to keep the per-task cost as low as possible","D":"Rotate models at random each time so the usage is spread evenly across all of them"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Model selection is about fit: simple, high-volume tasks run well on Haiku or Sonnet, while Opus is reserved for the hardest reasoning. Matching model to task controls cost and latency without sacrificing needed quality.","explanationDistractor":"\"Always use the largest model\" pays premium cost and latency for trivial work that a smaller model handles just as correctly.","reference":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview"},{"id":"as-016","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Prompt Structure","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"You want Claude to review a draft email the way an experienced communications editor would. Which addition to your prompt most directly sets that up?","options":{"A":"Assign a clear role, like \"act as an experienced communications editor,\" and say what to review","B":"Simply ask it to \"be helpful and thorough\" and see what comes back","C":"Paste the email with no other instructions and let Claude decide what to do","D":"Ask for the longest, most detailed response that Claude can possibly produce"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Giving Claude a specific role plus a clear task focuses its behavior and standards on the job; an editor's lens produces more targeted feedback than a vague request.","explanationDistractor":"\"Be helpful and thorough\" is too vague to steer the review; it gives Claude no concrete role, criteria, or focus to apply.","reference":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview"},{"id":"as-017","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Output Format","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"Claude keeps returning its answer as flowing paragraphs, but you need a table with three named columns to drop into a report. What is the most effective fix?","options":{"A":"Ask the same question again and hope for a table","B":"Switch to a larger, more capable model","C":"Tell Claude to \"make it look nicer\"","D":"Explicitly specify the output format: a table with the three exact column names you need"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Stating the desired output format precisely, a table with named columns, tells Claude exactly how to structure the response. Being explicit about format is the reliable way to get it.","explanationDistractor":"\"Make it look nicer\" is subjective and unspecified; Claude cannot reliably infer that you want a three-column table from it.","reference":null},{"id":"as-018","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Providing Context","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"You ask Claude to \"write a follow-up message to the client,\" but its draft misses that the client is upset about a missed deadline. What would most improve the result?","options":{"A":"Ask Claude to add a general apology somewhere near the opening","B":"Repeat the exact same request again, word for word","C":"Provide the context: the client's complaint, the missed deadline, and the outcome you want","D":"Ask Claude to make the message shorter and more direct"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Claude can only account for facts it is given. Supplying the situation, the client's concern, and your goal lets Claude write a genuinely responsive message instead of a generic one.","explanationDistractor":"Repeating the identical request gives Claude no new information, so the draft will keep missing the context it never received.","reference":null},{"id":"as-019","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Task Decomposition","scenario":"Quarterly Reporting","difficulty":"medium","stem":"You need Claude to (1) analyze survey data, (2) draft an executive summary, and (3) create a slide outline. A single prompt asking for all three at once yields shallow results on each. What is the better approach?","options":{"A":"Demand more detail in the same all-in-one prompt","B":"Break the work into sequential steps, doing and reviewing each before moving to the next","C":"Ask for all three again but request a larger font","D":"Drop two of the three tasks to simplify"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Decomposing a complex, multi-part request into focused steps lets Claude give each its full attention, and lets you check the output of one step before it feeds the next.","explanationDistractor":"Cramming more instructions into the same overloaded prompt keeps Claude splitting attention across three tasks at once, which is why each came out shallow.","reference":null},{"id":"as-020","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Diagnostic Iteration","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"An output is not quite right, so in one new attempt you rewrite the role, add three examples, change the format, and swap models all at once. It improves, but you have a problem. What is it?","options":{"A":"Nothing: changing everything at once is the fastest path","B":"You should have changed even more at the same time","C":"The model is now permanently biased by the earlier attempt","D":"You changed too many variables at once, so you cannot tell which change actually helped"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Diagnostic iteration means changing one variable at a time and observing the effect. Altering many things simultaneously makes the improvement uninterpretable and hard to reproduce.","explanationDistractor":"Changing even more at once compounds the problem: you would have zero ability to attribute the result to any specific change.","reference":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview"},{"id":"as-021","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Being Specific","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"Your prompts often produce vague, off-target answers. Which single change tends to help the most?","options":{"A":"Making the prompt longer by adding polite filler","B":"Asking Claude to \"be thorough\"","C":"Being specific about what you want: the exact scope, format, and criteria","D":"Sending the request several more times"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Specificity is the highest-leverage prompting habit: stating the exact scope, format, and success criteria removes the guesswork that produces vague answers.","explanationDistractor":"Telling Claude to \"be thorough\" adds no concrete direction; it does not tell Claude what to cover or how to shape the answer.","reference":null},{"id":"as-022","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Few-Shot Examples","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You need every product description formatted in a very specific pattern, and written instructions keep producing near-misses. What most reliably locks in the exact format?","options":{"A":"Provide two or three worked examples of the input and the exact output you want (few-shot)","B":"Add the word \"exactly\" to the instruction and repeat it","C":"Ask Claude to try harder and be more careful next time","D":"Make the instruction paragraph noticeably longer and more detailed"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"When a format is hard to convey in words, showing two or three concrete input-to-output examples (few-shot) demonstrates the pattern directly and is more reliable than more prose.","explanationDistractor":"Adding emphasis words like \"exactly\" does not clarify an ambiguous format; Claude still has to guess the pattern you never actually showed it.","reference":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview"},{"id":"as-023","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Examples vs Instructions","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Claude keeps misinterpreting how you want a data field transformed, even after you reworded the instruction twice. What is the most effective next move?","options":{"A":"Reword the instruction a third time","B":"Tell Claude it is wrong and to fix it","C":"Switch the output to all capital letters","D":"Show a concrete before-and-after example of the transformation you want"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"When instructions keep being misread, a concrete input-to-output example removes the ambiguity by demonstrating the exact transformation, which is often clearer than any wording.","explanationDistractor":"Rewording the instruction a third time is more of the same approach that already failed twice; a demonstration communicates what words could not.","reference":null},{"id":"as-024","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Prompt Structure","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A colleague's prompts are just a one-line task with no other detail, and results are inconsistent. Which set of additions best reflects good prompt structure?","options":{"A":"Louder wording, capital letters, and more exclamation points","B":"A clear role, the task, relevant context, the desired output format, and an example or two","C":"A higher maximum-token setting and a request for the longest answer","D":"Several unrelated questions bundled together into one message"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Well-structured prompts typically combine role, a clear task, relevant context, an explicit output format, and examples. Together these remove ambiguity and steer Claude toward the intended result.","explanationDistractor":"Bundling several unrelated questions together fragments Claude's focus and makes the prompt harder to answer well, not easier.","reference":null},{"id":"as-025","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Positive Instructions","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You keep telling Claude \"don't be so formal,\" but the tone still misses. Which instruction is likely to work better?","options":{"A":"Describe the tone you do want, warm and conversational like advice to a colleague, with a short example","B":"Repeat the instruction \"don't use any formal words at all, ever\" more emphatically","C":"Tell Claude firmly to \"stop being formal\" and leave it at that","D":"Ask Claude to simply \"be less of everything\" going forward"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Positively describing the target, the tone you do want with an example, gives Claude something concrete to aim at, which steers behavior better than a vague prohibition.","explanationDistractor":"\"Stop being formal\" says what to avoid but not what to do instead, leaving Claude to guess at the tone you actually want.","reference":null},{"id":"as-026","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Providing Context","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"You want Claude to answer questions using your company's internal policy, which it was never trained on. What is the essential step?","options":{"A":"Assume Claude already knows your internal policy","B":"Ask Claude to imagine what the policy probably says","C":"Use a bigger model so it \"knows more\"","D":"Provide the policy text as context (paste it or attach it) so Claude answers from it"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Claude has no knowledge of your private, internal documents unless you supply them. Giving it the actual policy text as context is what lets it answer accurately from your source.","explanationDistractor":"A bigger model has more general capability but still has never seen your internal policy; capability cannot substitute for the missing document.","reference":null},{"id":"as-027","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Being Specific","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A teammate asks Claude to \"make this report better\" and is frustrated by the results. What advice best addresses the root problem?","options":{"A":"Ask Claude to define \"better\" for you","B":"Run the request on every model and compare","C":"Tell Claude what \"better\" means here: clearer structure, shorter, more data-driven, and so on","D":"Accept that Claude cannot meaningfully improve reports"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"\"Better\" is subjective; Claude cannot read your mind about which dimension you care about. Naming the specific improvements you want turns an ambiguous ask into an actionable one.","explanationDistractor":"Asking Claude to define \"better\" just outsources your own criteria; it may optimize for a dimension you did not actually care about.","reference":null},{"id":"as-028","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Few-Shot Examples","scenario":null,"difficulty":"hard","stem":"For a straightforward task like \"summarize this paragraph in one sentence,\" Claude already does exactly what you want. When is adding few-shot examples actually worth the effort?","options":{"A":"Always add examples to every prompt, no matter how simple","B":"Never add examples; they only confuse the model","C":"When the task has a specific format or edge cases that plain instructions keep getting wrong","D":"Only when using the smallest available model"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Few-shot examples pay off when a task has a particular format or tricky edge cases that instructions alone do not nail. For simple tasks Claude already handles, the extra examples add little.","explanationDistractor":"Adding examples to every prompt regardless of need is wasted effort and token cost when a plain instruction already yields exactly what you want.","reference":null},{"id":"as-029","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Diagnostic Iteration","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Your first prompt gives a decent but too-technical answer for a general audience. What is the most efficient single adjustment to try next?","options":{"A":"Rewrite the entire prompt from scratch with many changes at once","B":"Switch entry points from chat to the API","C":"Ask the same prompt three more times","D":"Add one instruction specifying the audience and reading level, then compare the result"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Making one targeted change, specifying the audience, and observing the effect is efficient diagnostic iteration: you learn exactly whether that lever fixed the issue.","explanationDistractor":"Rewriting everything at once may fix it, but you lose the ability to tell which change mattered, making the next iteration harder.","reference":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview"},{"id":"as-030","domain":"prompting","studyArea":"Prompt Structure","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A prompt reads simply \"Write a job description,\" and the drafts come back generic and unusable. Which elements are most important to add?","options":{"A":"The role, the responsibilities, the required skills, the sections to include, and the tone","B":"An emphatic tone, bold text, and several exclamation points for energy","C":"A request for the maximum possible length and lots of detail","D":"A firm demand that Claude not make any mistakes this time"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"A generic draft is usually a symptom of a generic prompt. Supplying the specific role, responsibilities, required skills, structure, and tone gives Claude the substance to produce something usable.","explanationDistractor":"Demanding that Claude \"not make mistakes\" provides none of the concrete details, role, skills, or structure, that the draft is actually missing.","reference":null},{"id":"as-031","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Verifying Citations","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Claude's answer includes three specific statistics attributed to named studies. Before you use them in a published report, what should you do?","options":{"A":"Independently verify each statistic, and that the cited studies actually say it","B":"Trust them, since Claude cited specific sources","C":"Publish first and correct later if someone complains","D":"Assume any confidently stated number is reliable"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Claude can produce fluent, specific-looking citations that are inaccurate or fabricated. For anything you publish, verify each factual claim and citation against the primary source before relying on it.","explanationDistractor":"A confident, specific citation is not evidence of accuracy; Claude can state a fabricated source just as fluently as a real one, so trusting it unverified is risky.","reference":null},{"id":"as-032","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Confident but Wrong","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"An answer is written with total confidence and reads smoothly, with no hedging. What can you conclude about its accuracy?","options":{"A":"Confident, fluent writing means it is almost certainly correct","B":"Fluency and confidence say nothing about accuracy; it can be confidently wrong","C":"The lack of hedging proves it was fact-checked","D":"Smooth writing indicates the model used web search"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"The tone and fluency of an answer are independent of its correctness. Claude can be confidently and articulately wrong, so confidence is not a reliability signal.","explanationDistractor":"Absence of hedging is a stylistic feature, not a fact-check; it does not mean the claims were verified against any source.","reference":null},{"id":"as-033","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Hallucinations","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You ask Claude for case law supporting an argument, and it returns a citation with a court, year, and quote that looks authoritative, but you cannot find the case anywhere. What is the most likely explanation?","options":{"A":"The case is real but too obscure to appear anywhere","B":"Your search skills are the problem; the citation is surely valid","C":"Claude may have hallucinated a plausible-looking but nonexistent citation","D":"The court must have removed the case from public records"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Models can generate citations that look authoritative but do not exist, a hallucination. If a specific, checkable source cannot be found, treat it as unverified rather than assuming it is merely obscure.","explanationDistractor":"Assuming the citation must be valid and blaming your search ignores a well-known failure mode: confidently formatted citations that were never real.","reference":null},{"id":"as-034","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Human Verification","scenario":"Clinical Guidance","difficulty":"medium","stem":"Claude drafts what looks like sound guidance on adjusting a patient's medication dosage. What is the responsible way to use this output?","options":{"A":"Follow it directly, since the reasoning is detailed","B":"Share it with the patient as a definitive answer","C":"Use it only if it happens to agree with your initial hunch","D":"Treat it as a draft to be reviewed and approved by a qualified medical professional"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Medical dosing is high-stakes and potentially irreversible, so it requires human expert verification. Claude's output should be treated as a draft for a qualified professional to review, never a final clinical decision.","explanationDistractor":"Following detailed-looking reasoning directly skips the professional judgment that high-stakes medical decisions require; detail is not the same as clinical validation.","reference":null},{"id":"as-035","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Irreversible Actions","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"Claude generates a database command that will permanently delete every record matching a filter. What should you do before running it?","options":{"A":"Review and verify exactly what it will delete before executing an irreversible action","B":"Run it immediately to save time","C":"Run it on the production database first, then check","D":"Assume it is safe because Claude wrote it"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Irreversible actions demand human verification first. Reviewing precisely what the command affects before executing catches mistakes while they are still reversible.","explanationDistractor":"Assuming the command is safe because Claude produced it skips the check that matters most, exactly when the consequences cannot be undone.","reference":null},{"id":"as-036","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Bias","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You use Claude to screen resumes and notice its shortlist skews heavily toward one demographic. What is the appropriate response?","options":{"A":"Trust the output; the model is objective by design","B":"Treat it as a possible bias signal, examine the criteria, and add human review","C":"Assume the applicant pool simply looked that way","D":"Remove the human reviewers to speed things up"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"AI outputs can reflect and amplify bias. A skewed result is a signal to scrutinize the criteria and keep human oversight, especially for consequential decisions about people.","explanationDistractor":"Treating the model as inherently objective ignores that it can reproduce bias; a skewed shortlist warrants investigation, not blind trust.","reference":null},{"id":"as-037","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Verifying Against Source","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Claude summarizes a long report and includes a striking claim you do not remember reading. Before quoting that claim, what is the right step?","options":{"A":"Quote it; summaries are reliable by nature","B":"Rephrase it so it sounds less striking","C":"Check the claim against the source document to confirm it is actually there","D":"Delete the whole summary to be safe"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Summaries can introduce claims that are not in the source. Verifying a surprising claim against the original document guards against repeating something Claude inferred or fabricated.","explanationDistractor":"Rephrasing a claim to sound less striking does nothing to establish whether it is actually supported by the source.","reference":null},{"id":"as-038","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Scaling Verification","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"For which task is it most reasonable to accept Claude's output with only a light check?","options":{"A":"Calculating the correct dosage for a prescription","B":"Drafting the binding wording of a legal contract","C":"Confirming a specific historical date for a published article","D":"Brainstorming a list of possible names for an internal project"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Verification effort should scale with stakes. A low-stakes, easily reversible, non-factual task like brainstorming internal names carries little risk if a suggestion is imperfect.","explanationDistractor":"A binding legal contract is high-stakes and hard to reverse; its wording needs careful human review, not a light check.","reference":null},{"id":"as-039","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Accountability","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"You are about to send a client-facing analysis that Claude largely wrote, under your own name. What is the guiding principle?","options":{"A":"You are accountable for it, so validate the facts and reasoning before it goes out","B":"Claude wrote it, so any errors are not your responsibility","C":"Client-facing work needs no more care than internal notes","D":"Send it now; clients expect occasional errors"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"When you put your name on Claude's output, you own its accuracy. Validating the facts and reasoning before it goes out is the core responsibility of using AI in real work.","explanationDistractor":"Disclaiming responsibility because \"Claude wrote it\" does not hold up; the person who sends the work is accountable for it.","reference":null},{"id":"as-040","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Fabricated Precision","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Asked for market-size figures, Claude returns very precise numbers such as \"$4.37B in 2023\" with no source. How should you treat them?","options":{"A":"Use them as-is; the precision signals reliability","B":"Treat them as unverified estimates and confirm against a real source before using","C":"Round them off, which makes them accurate","D":"Assume Claude retrieved them from a live database"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Oddly precise figures with no source are a classic hallucination pattern. Precision is not provenance; confirm the numbers against an authoritative source before relying on them.","explanationDistractor":"Precision does not signal reliability; a fabricated figure can be stated to the cent just as easily as a rounded one.","reference":null},{"id":"as-041","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Checking Accuracy","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A quick way to gauge whether a long Claude answer is trustworthy on a topic you do not know well is to:","options":{"A":"Count how many sources it mentions","B":"See whether it sounds confident throughout","C":"Spot-check a few claims you can verify against known facts or sources","D":"Measure how long the response is"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Spot-checking verifiable claims gives real evidence about accuracy. If the checkable parts are wrong, that is a strong signal to distrust the parts you cannot immediately verify.","explanationDistractor":"The number of sources mentioned says nothing about whether those sources are real or support the claims; it is easy to list citations that do not check out.","reference":null},{"id":"as-042","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Human Verification","scenario":"Vendor Contract Review","difficulty":"medium","stem":"Claude reviews a vendor contract and flags no issues, and your company is about to sign. What is the responsible next step?","options":{"A":"Sign immediately, since Claude found nothing","B":"Skip legal review to save time and money","C":"Assume no flags means no legal risk","D":"Have a qualified lawyer review it before signing a binding agreement"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"A binding contract is high-stakes and hard to reverse. Claude's review can assist, but a qualified legal professional must verify before you commit; \"no flags\" is not legal clearance.","explanationDistractor":"Treating \"Claude found nothing\" as proof of no legal risk over-trusts a tool that can miss issues and is not a substitute for professional legal judgment.","reference":null},{"id":"as-043","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Over-Trust","scenario":null,"difficulty":"hard","stem":"A team has used Claude for months with good results and now approves its outputs without any review. What is the main risk?","options":{"A":"Errors will slip through unnoticed precisely because no one is checking anymore","B":"The model will run out of capacity","C":"Reviewing was never necessary in the first place","D":"Claude will refuse to answer without a reviewer present"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"A good track record does not make outputs infallible. Dropping verification entirely means the inevitable errors reach production with nothing to catch them; appropriate review should remain.","explanationDistractor":"Concluding review was never necessary misreads past success; the good results likely depended in part on the very checking now being removed.","reference":null},{"id":"as-044","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Verifying Citations","scenario":null,"difficulty":"hard","stem":"Claude provides a real, working URL as a citation for a claim. What still needs checking?","options":{"A":"Nothing; a working link fully validates the claim","B":"That the linked source actually says what the claim attributes to it","C":"Only that the link loads quickly","D":"Only the website's visual design"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"A valid link is necessary but not sufficient. Claude may cite a real page that does not actually support the specific claim, so you must confirm the source says what is attributed to it.","explanationDistractor":"A link merely loading proves the page exists, not that its content backs the claim it was attached to.","reference":null},{"id":"as-045","domain":"evaluating","studyArea":"Scaling Verification","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"Which output most clearly demands independent human verification before you act on it?","options":{"A":"A haiku Claude wrote for a birthday card","B":"A list of icebreaker questions for a casual meeting","C":"A suggested color palette for a slide deck","D":"A tax-filing figure Claude calculated that you will submit to the authorities"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Verification scales with stakes and reversibility. A tax figure you will formally submit is high-stakes, factual, and consequential if wrong, so it demands independent verification, unlike creative, low-risk outputs.","explanationDistractor":"A haiku for a birthday card is low-stakes and subjective; an error carries no real consequence, so it does not require the same scrutiny.","reference":null},{"id":"as-046","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Delegation Boundaries","scenario":"Monthly Reporting","difficulty":"medium","stem":"You are deciding which parts of a monthly reporting process to hand to Claude. Which step is the best candidate to delegate?","options":{"A":"Reformatting the raw numbers into the standard report template, a repeatable and checkable step","B":"Deciding whether to lay off part of the team based on what the numbers show","C":"Choosing the company's confidential strategic priorities for the year","D":"Signing off on the final figures that will be submitted to the board"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"The best tasks to delegate are well-specified, repeatable, and easy to verify. Reformatting numbers into a template fits all three; the judgment-heavy, accountability-bearing steps stay with a human.","explanationDistractor":"Deciding whether to lay off a team is a high-stakes judgment call with human accountability, exactly the kind of step to keep with a person.","reference":null},{"id":"as-047","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Human Judgment","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"Which task should stay with a human rather than being fully delegated to Claude?","options":{"A":"Converting a bulleted list into a formatted table","B":"Making the final call on a sensitive personnel decision","C":"Drafting a first version of routine meeting notes","D":"Suggesting synonyms for a marketing headline"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Ambiguous, high-stakes decisions that carry human accountability, like sensitive personnel calls, should remain human judgments. Claude can inform them but should not own them.","explanationDistractor":"Converting a list to a table is a mechanical, low-risk transformation that is a fine fit for delegation, unlike a consequential personnel decision.","reference":null},{"id":"as-048","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Communicating Limits","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You are presenting a new Claude-assisted workflow to leadership. How should you describe its capabilities?","options":{"A":"Promise it is fully autonomous and error-free to build confidence","B":"Avoid mentioning any limitations so the project gets approved","C":"Present both the value and the limitations honestly, including where human review is still required","D":"Claim it will replace the whole team immediately"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Responsible adoption depends on setting accurate expectations. Communicating both the value and the limitations, including where humans stay in the loop, builds durable trust and prevents overreliance.","explanationDistractor":"Promising an error-free, fully autonomous system oversells the tool and sets up the project to lose credibility the first time it makes a mistake.","reference":null},{"id":"as-049","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Team Adoption","scenario":"Team Enablement","difficulty":"medium","stem":"You personally get great results from Claude, but your teammates get inconsistent ones on the same tasks. What best moves the team from \"I use Claude\" to \"our workflow uses Claude\"?","options":{"A":"Keep your prompting techniques to yourself as a personal edge","B":"Tell teammates to simply try harder","C":"Let everyone figure it out independently","D":"Capture the working prompts, context, and configuration in a shared setup others can reuse"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Scaling from individual to team use means turning personal know-how into shared, reusable assets: documented prompts, shared Project instructions, and knowledge, so everyone gets consistent results.","explanationDistractor":"Leaving each teammate to figure it out independently reproduces the very inconsistency you are trying to solve; the knowledge stays trapped with one person.","reference":null},{"id":"as-050","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"First Candidates","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A team wants a first win with Claude. Which process is the best starting candidate?","options":{"A":"A frequent, well-defined, low-risk task with clear inputs and checkable outputs","B":"The most ambiguous, high-stakes decision the team faces","C":"A rare one-off task that will never recur","D":"A process no one currently understands"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Good first candidates are frequent, well-defined, and low-risk with verifiable outputs: they deliver clear value, are easy to validate, and build confidence before tackling harder work.","explanationDistractor":"Starting with the most ambiguous, high-stakes decision maximizes risk and the chance of a visible failure, undermining early adoption.","reference":null},{"id":"as-051","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Communicating Value","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"How should you frame the value of a Claude-assisted drafting workflow to stakeholders?","options":{"A":"\"It eliminates the need for any human writers on the team entirely.\"","B":"\"It produces solid first drafts fast, which we then review and refine to keep quality high.\"","C":"\"It is essentially perfect and its drafts never need any editing.\"","D":"\"It will do everything the team currently does, only instantly and at no cost.\""},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Honest value framing pairs the real benefit (faster first drafts, less turnaround) with the human role that preserves quality. It is credible and sustainable, unlike overclaiming.","explanationDistractor":"Claiming the output is perfect and never needs editing sets an expectation the tool cannot meet and erodes trust at the first necessary edit.","reference":null},{"id":"as-052","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Accountability","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"In a Claude-assisted approval workflow, who should hold accountability for the final decision?","options":{"A":"The model, since it produced the recommendation","B":"No one in particular, as long as it works","C":"A designated human owner who reviews and approves the outcome","D":"Whichever teammate happens to be least busy that day"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Accountability must rest with a person. A designated human owner who reviews and approves keeps responsibility clear and ensures a competent check on consequential outcomes.","explanationDistractor":"Assigning accountability to the model is a category error; a tool cannot be answerable for a decision, so a named human must own it.","reference":null},{"id":"as-053","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Mixed Workflows","scenario":"Hiring Pipeline","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A hiring workflow has four steps: (1) format incoming resumes, (2) draft interview questions, (3) decide who advances, and (4) send templated scheduling emails. Which split is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"Delegate all four steps entirely to Claude with no human review","B":"Keep all four steps fully manual and do not use Claude at all","C":"Delegate only the who-advances decision to Claude and do the rest by hand","D":"Delegate formatting, drafting, and templated emails; keep the advancement decision human"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Delegate the well-specified, low-judgment steps (formatting, drafting, templated sends) and reserve the consequential, accountability-bearing decision, who advances, for a human.","explanationDistractor":"Delegating the hiring decision itself hands a high-stakes judgment about people to a tool, which is exactly the step that should stay human.","reference":null},{"id":"as-054","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Durability","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Your Claude-assisted workflow works well, but only you know how to run it. What most improves its long-term resilience?","options":{"A":"Documenting the steps, prompts, and configuration so others can run it if you are away","B":"Keeping it all in your head to stay indispensable","C":"Deleting your notes once it works","D":"Rebuilding it from scratch each month"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"A workflow that lives only in one person's head is fragile. Documenting the steps, prompts, and configuration lets the team operate it reliably even when the original author is unavailable.","explanationDistractor":"Keeping it all in your head makes you a single point of failure; the workflow breaks the moment you are unavailable.","reference":null},{"id":"as-055","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Managing Expectations","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A stakeholder expects the new Claude workflow to be \"100% accurate with no oversight.\" What is the best response?","options":{"A":"Agree, to keep them happy","B":"Clarify that some human review remains necessary and explain where errors are most likely","C":"Quietly remove all review steps to match their expectation","D":"Tell them accuracy is not measurable"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Managing expectations honestly, that review is still needed and where errors tend to occur, protects the project and the stakeholder from an unrealistic promise the tool cannot keep.","explanationDistractor":"Agreeing to \"100% accurate with no oversight\" commits to a standard no AI workflow can guarantee, setting up an inevitable, trust-damaging failure.","reference":null},{"id":"as-056","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Communicating Value","scenario":null,"difficulty":"hard","stem":"You want to show that adopting Claude improved a workflow. What is the most credible way to communicate the value?","options":{"A":"Assert broadly that everything is just \"so much better now\" than before","B":"Share a single dramatic success anecdote and generalize from that one case","C":"Compare before-and-after measures like turnaround time and rework rate, noting the human steps kept","D":"Claim sweeping benefits that cannot really be measured or verified"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Credible value communication uses concrete, comparable measures (time, rework) and is honest about what still requires people. It gives stakeholders something real to evaluate.","explanationDistractor":"A single dramatic anecdote is not representative and can mislead; one story does not establish that the workflow reliably improved.","reference":null},{"id":"as-057","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Delegation Boundaries","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"When deciding whether Claude can take an action automatically or a human must approve it first, which factor matters most?","options":{"A":"How quickly the action can be completed","B":"How impressive the automation looks to others","C":"Whether the model seems to handle it confidently","D":"How reversible the action is and how costly a mistake would be"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"The key delegation test is the cost and reversibility of error: cheap, reversible actions can be automated, while costly or irreversible ones warrant human approval first.","explanationDistractor":"Speed is a benefit, not a safety criterion; automating an irreversible, high-cost action just because it is fast invites serious harm.","reference":null},{"id":"as-058","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Solution Fit","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A colleague proposes building an elaborate automated system for a task the team does twice a year by hand in ten minutes. What is the most sensible guidance?","options":{"A":"Match the investment to the need; a heavyweight system is overkill for a rare, quick task","B":"Always build the most fully automated solution that is technically possible","C":"Automate it thoroughly precisely because the task is so infrequent","D":"Add as many features as possible up front so nothing is missing later"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Solution design should fit the problem. For a rare, quick task, the overhead of an elaborate system outweighs any benefit; a simple manual approach (or a light assist) is more sensible.","explanationDistractor":"Building the most automated solution regardless of need wastes effort on a twice-a-year task and creates a system to maintain for little return.","reference":null},{"id":"as-059","domain":"workflow","studyArea":"Human-in-the-Loop","scenario":"Refund Processing","difficulty":"medium","stem":"You are integrating Claude into a customer-refund process. Refunds under $20 are routine; refunds over $500 are sensitive. How should you place the human checkpoint?","options":{"A":"Require human approval for every refund, including $2 ones","B":"Let Claude approve all refunds of any size automatically","C":"Require human approval only for the smallest refunds","D":"Auto-process small routine refunds and route large or unusual ones to a human"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Place human review where the stakes are: automate the low-risk, routine refunds and escalate the large or unusual ones. This concentrates human attention where errors are costly.","explanationDistractor":"Letting Claude auto-approve refunds of any size removes oversight exactly where it matters most: the large, sensitive payouts.","reference":null},{"id":"as-060","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Custom Instructions","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"In a Claude Project, you set custom instructions that say \"always write in British English and cite the source section.\" What is the effect?","options":{"A":"Every chat started in that Project follows those instructions automatically","B":"Only the very next message follows them","C":"The instructions apply to all Projects you own","D":"The instructions apply only after you repeat them each chat"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"A Project's custom instructions persist and apply to every conversation started within that Project, so shared preferences are enforced automatically without re-stating them.","explanationDistractor":"Custom instructions are not limited to a single message; the whole point is that they persist across every chat in the Project.","reference":null},{"id":"as-061","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Knowledge Sources","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"Your team keeps asking Claude questions about a 60-page internal handbook. What is the durable way to make that handbook available in every relevant chat?","options":{"A":"Paste the whole handbook at the start of each new chat","B":"Add the handbook as a knowledge source in a Project so chats can draw on it","C":"Ask Claude to memorize it once and rely on that","D":"Summarize it from memory each time it comes up"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Attaching the handbook as a Project knowledge source makes it available to every chat in the Project, so Claude can ground answers in it without anyone re-pasting the document.","explanationDistractor":"Asking Claude to \"memorize\" a document once does not work; it has no persistent memory of pasted text across separate conversations.","reference":null},{"id":"as-062","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Keeping Config Current","scenario":"Pricing Knowledge Base","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A Project's knowledge base still contains last year's pricing, and Claude keeps quoting the old prices. What is the root-cause fix?","options":{"A":"Tell users to mentally adjust the prices themselves","B":"Add a note in each chat correcting the price","C":"Update the knowledge source to the current pricing so every chat inherits the correct data","D":"Switch to a larger model"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Stale configuration is a leading cause of wrong output. Updating the Project's knowledge source to current pricing fixes the problem at its root for every future chat, rather than patching each conversation.","explanationDistractor":"Correcting the price by hand in each chat treats the symptom repeatedly while the outdated source keeps producing the same error.","reference":null},{"id":"as-063","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Project Context","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"What is the main advantage of putting durable context into a Project rather than into individual prompts?","options":{"A":"It mainly just makes each individual prompt take longer to type out","B":"It hides the shared context from the people who are using the Project","C":"It helps only the very first chat you create and none of the ones after","D":"Every chat in the Project inherits that context, so quality and consistency improve"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Project context is shared by every conversation in the Project. Investing in good instructions and knowledge once raises the baseline quality and consistency of all chats that follow.","explanationDistractor":"Project context is not limited to the first chat; its value is precisely that it carries into every conversation in the Project.","reference":null},{"id":"as-064","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Instructions vs Prompt","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Which item is best placed in a Project's custom instructions rather than typed into a single prompt?","options":{"A":"A standing rule that all outputs use the company's four-part report structure","B":"A one-time question about today's weather","C":"A throwaway request to rephrase a single sentence","D":"A personal note unrelated to the Project's purpose"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Durable, always-apply rules, like a standard report structure every output should follow, belong in custom instructions so they take effect automatically in every chat.","explanationDistractor":"A one-time weather question is transient and specific to one conversation; putting it in standing instructions would wrongly apply it to every future chat.","reference":null},{"id":"as-065","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Knowledge vs Instructions","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You have (1) reference documents Claude should consult and (2) rules about tone and format. In a Project, where does each belong?","options":{"A":"Put both the documents and the rules into the custom instructions","B":"Reference documents as knowledge sources, tone and format rules as custom instructions","C":"Put both the documents and the rules in as knowledge sources","D":"Neither the documents nor the rules belong in a Project at all"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Knowledge sources hold reference material Claude draws on; custom instructions hold standing behavioral rules like tone and format. Matching each to its slot keeps the Project organized and effective.","explanationDistractor":"Pasting large reference documents into the instructions field misuses it; bulky reference material belongs in knowledge sources, with instructions reserved for rules.","reference":null},{"id":"as-066","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Keeping Config Current","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Your company just changed its refund policy, and several Projects rely on the old policy document. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Leave the documents; Claude will figure out the new policy on its own","B":"Wait until someone complains about a wrong answer","C":"Update the policy document in the affected Projects so chats reflect the new rules","D":"Delete the Projects and start over from nothing"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Configuration must be kept current as facts change. Proactively updating the policy document in the affected Projects prevents Claude from confidently applying outdated rules.","explanationDistractor":"Waiting for a complaint means Claude keeps giving wrong, outdated answers in the meantime; keeping config current is a proactive maintenance task.","reference":null},{"id":"as-067","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Project Scoping","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You do two very different kinds of work: legal contract review and social-media copywriting. How should you organize Projects for the best context in each?","options":{"A":"Put everything in one Project so all context is always available","B":"Use no Projects and paste context each time","C":"Randomly assign chats to whichever Project is open","D":"Create a separate Project for each, with its own instructions and knowledge tailored to that work"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Separate Projects keep each body of instructions and knowledge focused and relevant. Tailored context per Project prevents the legal rules and the copywriting rules from bleeding into the wrong chats.","explanationDistractor":"Cramming both kinds of work into one Project mixes unrelated instructions and knowledge, diluting the context and risking cross-contamination of guidance.","reference":null},{"id":"as-068","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Team Consistency","scenario":"Team Onboarding","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A new hire's Claude outputs do not match the team's house style, while veterans' outputs do. What is the most scalable fix?","options":{"A":"Give the team a shared Project whose instructions and knowledge encode the house style, so everyone starts the same","B":"Have the new hire shadow a veteran for a full month before they are allowed to use Claude","C":"Ask the new hire to infer the house style on their own from past documents","D":"Accept that new hires will always be off-style for their first while"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Encoding the house style once in a shared Project gives every team member, new or veteran, the same durable context, making consistent output the default rather than something each person recreates.","explanationDistractor":"Having the new hire guess the style from old documents is slow and unreliable, and it leaves the knowledge uncaptured for the next new hire.","reference":null},{"id":"as-069","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Config Hygiene","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"What is a good practice for Project knowledge that references facts, policies, or data that change over time?","options":{"A":"Set it once and never look at it again","B":"Review and refresh it on a regular cadence so it stays accurate","C":"Assume Claude will update it automatically","D":"Only update it after a major public incident"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Knowledge that references changeable facts drifts out of date. Reviewing and refreshing it on a regular cadence keeps every chat grounded in current information.","explanationDistractor":"Claude does not automatically update your uploaded knowledge sources; keeping them current is the owner's responsibility.","reference":null},{"id":"as-070","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Grounding","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Without any reference material, Claude sometimes guesses at your product's specifications. How does a well-configured Project reduce this?","options":{"A":"It mainly makes Claude sound more confident when it is guessing","B":"It disables Claude's ability to answer questions about the product","C":"It supplies the real specifications as knowledge, so Claude answers from your source","D":"It has no real effect on the accuracy of the answers either way"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Providing the actual specifications as Project knowledge grounds Claude's answers in your real data, reducing the guessing that leads to inaccurate claims about your product.","explanationDistractor":"A Project does not make guessing more confident; it replaces guessing with grounded answers by giving Claude the real reference material.","reference":null},{"id":"as-071","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Instruction Specificity","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A Project's custom instruction reads only \"be professional,\" yet outputs vary widely in structure and length. What would most improve consistency?","options":{"A":"Remove the instruction entirely","B":"Add more vague adjectives like \"good\" and \"nice\"","C":"Switch models for each chat","D":"Make the instruction specific: define the structure, length, and tone you expect"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Vague instructions produce varied results. Specifying the concrete structure, length, and tone gives every chat a precise target, which is what drives consistency.","explanationDistractor":"Adding more vague adjectives compounds the ambiguity; \"good\" and \"nice\" give Claude no more concrete direction than \"professional\" did.","reference":null},{"id":"as-072","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Single Source of Truth","scenario":null,"difficulty":"hard","stem":"Different team members keep their own slightly different copies of the prompt instructions, and outputs have drifted apart. What is the best remedy?","options":{"A":"Let each person keep refining their private version","B":"Consolidate into one shared Project configuration that everyone uses as the source of truth","C":"Email a new instruction sheet every week","D":"Stop giving Claude any instructions at all"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Divergent private copies cause drift. A single shared Project configuration acts as the source of truth so everyone works from the same, maintained instructions and knowledge.","explanationDistractor":"Letting each person refine a private version is what produced the drift; it guarantees the copies keep diverging.","reference":null},{"id":"as-073","domain":"configuration","studyArea":"Knowledge Currency","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A Project's knowledge contains three versions of the same policy: last year's, a draft, and the current one. Claude sometimes cites the wrong version. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Add a fourth, combined version to be safe","B":"Tell users to specify the version in every question","C":"Leave all three; more documents is always better","D":"Remove the outdated and draft versions, keeping only the current authoritative one"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Conflicting documents let Claude cite the wrong one. Keeping only the current, authoritative version removes the ambiguity at the source so every chat references the right policy.","explanationDistractor":"\"More documents is always better\" is false when they conflict; extra outdated versions are exactly what causes Claude to cite the wrong policy.","reference":null},{"id":"as-074","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Secrets","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"While debugging, you are about to paste a configuration file that includes a live production API key into a chat. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Remove or redact the secret before sharing; never paste live credentials","B":"Paste it as-is; it speeds up debugging","C":"Paste it but ask Claude to ignore the key","D":"Paste it now and rotate the key next quarter"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Live credentials should never be pasted into external tools. Redact or remove the secret first; asking a tool to \"ignore\" a key does not undo the exposure.","explanationDistractor":"Asking Claude to \"ignore\" the key does nothing to protect it; the secret has still left your controlled environment the moment it is pasted.","reference":null},{"id":"as-075","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Regulated Data","scenario":"Customer Health Data","difficulty":"medium","stem":"You want to analyze a spreadsheet of customers' names, addresses, and health conditions. What is the responsible first step?","options":{"A":"Paste it immediately; analysis is harmless","B":"Check your organization's data policy and get authorization before using regulated personal data","C":"Assume it is fine because the data is only being summarized","D":"Remove just the addresses and paste the rest"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Names tied to health conditions are sensitive, regulated personal data. Responsible use requires following your organization's data policy and obtaining authorization before putting such data into any tool.","explanationDistractor":"Removing only the addresses still leaves names linked to health conditions, highly sensitive PII that its own governance requirements apply to.","reference":null},{"id":"as-076","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Confidential Data","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A teammate suggests pasting an unreleased, confidential product roadmap into a tool that is not approved for confidential data. What is the right response?","options":{"A":"Go ahead and paste it; a roadmap is not really that secret","B":"Paste a \"lightly edited\" version of it to be a bit safer","C":"Do not use an unapproved tool for confidential data; follow the policy on where it may go","D":"Paste it, then delete the chat afterward to clean up"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Confidential material should only go to tools approved for it. Understanding and following the policy on where sensitive data is allowed to flow is central to responsible use.","explanationDistractor":"Deleting the chat afterward does not retroactively make an unapproved destination compliant; the confidential data was already sent there.","reference":null},{"id":"as-077","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Where Data Goes","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Before entering any sensitive business data into an AI tool, the most important thing to understand is:","options":{"A":"How fast the tool responds","B":"How colorful the interface is","C":"Whether the output will be formatted as a table","D":"Where the data goes, how it may be used or retained, and whether your policy permits it"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Responsible use starts with data awareness: knowing where your input goes, how it may be used or retained, and whether policy allows it. That determines whether entering the data is appropriate at all.","explanationDistractor":"Response speed is a convenience factor, not a governance consideration; it tells you nothing about whether the data is safe or permitted to enter the tool.","reference":null},{"id":"as-078","domain":"governance","studyArea":"What Not to Paste","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"Which of the following is clearly inappropriate to paste into a general AI chat tool?","options":{"A":"A colleague's password and a customer's full credit-card number","B":"A publicly available product brochure","C":"A draft blog post you wrote for the public site","D":"A list of common English idioms"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Secrets and sensitive personal financial data, passwords and full card numbers, must never be entered into a general tool. The other items are already public or non-sensitive.","explanationDistractor":"A publicly available brochure carries no confidentiality or privacy risk, so it is not comparable to pasting passwords or card numbers.","reference":null},{"id":"as-079","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Data Minimization","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You need Claude to help spot trends in support tickets, but the tickets contain customer names and emails you do not need for the analysis. What is the best practice?","options":{"A":"Include all the personal details to be thorough","B":"Remove or anonymize the personal details you do not need before sending the data","C":"Include the emails but not the names","D":"Send everything and hope it is not retained"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Data minimization means sharing only what the task requires. Stripping or anonymizing personal details you do not need for trend analysis reduces exposure while still enabling the work.","explanationDistractor":"Keeping the emails but dropping the names still transmits personal contact data that the trend analysis does not require.","reference":null},{"id":"as-080","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Responsible Use","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A user asks Claude to help them access an ex-partner's private accounts without permission. As a responsible operator, what is the right stance?","options":{"A":"Help them out, since the user asked for it politely","B":"Help them, as long as it is technically feasible to do","C":"Decline; this is unauthorized access to someone else's private accounts, however it is framed","D":"Help, but add a disclaimer about using it responsibly"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Responsible use includes refusing tasks that facilitate harm or unauthorized access to others' private data. Neither politeness nor technical feasibility makes the request acceptable.","explanationDistractor":"Adding a disclaimer does not change the nature of the act; assisting unauthorized access to someone's private accounts is inappropriate regardless of a caveat.","reference":null},{"id":"as-081","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Data Classification","scenario":null,"difficulty":"hard","stem":"Your company classifies data as Public, Internal, Confidential, and Restricted. Before pasting a document into an AI tool, the classification matters because:","options":{"A":"Higher classifications always produce better answers","B":"Classification only affects file-size limits","C":"The color of the label changes the model's behavior","D":"It tells you the handling rules: some classes may not be entered into certain tools at all"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Data classification drives handling requirements. Knowing a document's class tells you whether, and in which tools, it may be used, which is the governance question that comes before any prompt.","explanationDistractor":"Classification is about handling rules and permitted destinations, not file-size limits; it governs whether the data may be entered at all.","reference":null},{"id":"as-082","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Health Data","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A clinic wants to use a general consumer AI tool to summarize identifiable patient records. Before doing so, the key governance question is:","options":{"A":"Whether that use is permitted under applicable health-privacy rules and the tool's approved handling","B":"Whether the generated summaries will each come in under 200 words","C":"Whether the clinic staff would prefer bullet points or flowing prose","D":"Whether the tool offers a dark mode for late-night shifts"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Identifiable patient records are protected health information. The governing question is whether the intended use complies with health-privacy rules and the tool's approved handling, not stylistic preferences.","explanationDistractor":"The length or formatting of the summaries is irrelevant to whether it is lawful and permitted to process identifiable patient data in that tool.","reference":null},{"id":"as-083","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Output Review","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Before forwarding a Claude-generated report to an external partner, you should:","options":{"A":"Send it right away; it was generated internally","B":"Review it to ensure no confidential or personal internal data was carried into the output","C":"Only check the spelling","D":"Assume Claude removed anything sensitive automatically"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Outputs can inadvertently include sensitive details drawn from your inputs. Reviewing before external sharing prevents leaking confidential or personal information outside the organization.","explanationDistractor":"Claude does not automatically know which of your internal details are confidential; assuming it stripped them is unsafe before an external send.","reference":null},{"id":"as-084","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Approved Tools","scenario":"Shadow IT","difficulty":"medium","stem":"An employee starts using a personal, unvetted AI account for work involving company data because it is convenient. What is the governance concern?","options":{"A":"There is none; convenience is really all that matters here","B":"Only that the personal account might be a little slower","C":"Company data may flow through an unapproved tool, outside the organization's controls","D":"Only that the tool's fonts and colors look a bit different"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Using unapproved, personal tools for company data routes sensitive information outside sanctioned controls, data-handling agreements, and oversight, a core governance risk regardless of convenience.","explanationDistractor":"Speed is not the issue; the real concern is company data leaving approved, governed channels through an unvetted personal account.","reference":null},{"id":"as-085","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Data Minimization","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Which principle best guides how much data to include when prompting with real business information?","options":{"A":"Include only the data necessary for the task, and no more","B":"Always include the maximum data available for context","C":"Include everything and let Claude decide what to ignore","D":"Include extra unrelated data to test the model"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Data minimization, sharing only what the task needs, limits exposure of sensitive information and is a foundational responsible-use practice.","explanationDistractor":"Dumping the maximum available data needlessly exposes sensitive information that the task never required, increasing risk with no benefit.","reference":null},{"id":"as-086","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Where Data Goes","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Two AI tools are available. Tool X is approved by your company with a data agreement in place; Tool Y is a random free site with unknown data practices. For confidential work, which should you use and why?","options":{"A":"Tool Y, because free is cheaper","B":"Either one; it makes no difference for confidential data","C":"Tool X, because its approved status and data agreement govern how your data is handled","D":"Whichever loads faster in your browser"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"For confidential work, the approved tool with a known data agreement is the responsible choice; you understand and have assurances about where the data goes and how it is handled.","explanationDistractor":"A free site with unknown data practices offers no assurances about retention or use, making it unsuitable for confidential data regardless of cost.","reference":null},{"id":"as-087","domain":"governance","studyArea":"Responsible-Use Policy","scenario":null,"difficulty":"hard","stem":"An organization wants a simple rule for employees about sensitive data and AI tools. Which rule is soundest?","options":{"A":"\"Move fast: any tool is fine as long as it helps you hit deadlines, and worry about data later.\"","B":"\"Never use AI tools for work; the risk of any data exposure always outweighs the benefit.\"","C":"\"Use any tool you like for sensitive data, but delete the conversation afterward to erase the exposure.\"","D":"\"Use only approved tools, follow the data-classification policy, and never enter secrets or regulated personal data without authorization.\""},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"A sound rule combines approved tools, adherence to data classification, and a clear prohibition on entering secrets or regulated personal data without authorization, covering the main governance risks concisely.","explanationDistractor":"Deleting the chat afterward (option C) does not reverse the exposure; the sensitive data has already left your controlled environment, so \"delete later\" is not real protection.","reference":null},{"id":"as-088","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Missing Context","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"Claude's answers to your question are consistently generic and could apply to any company. What is the most likely root cause and fix?","options":{"A":"The prompt lacks specific context; add the concrete details unique to your situation","B":"The model is broken; file a bug report","C":"You need to ask the same question more times","D":"The output length is set too high"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Generic output usually traces back to a generic, context-poor prompt. Supplying the specifics of your situation is the durable fix that makes answers relevant.","explanationDistractor":"Repeating the same context-free question just yields more generic answers; the missing ingredient is the specific context, not repetition.","reference":null},{"id":"as-089","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Format Fixes","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"You have clearly described the output format three times and Claude still gets the layout slightly wrong. What is the most effective durable fix?","options":{"A":"Describe the format a fourth time, more emphatically","B":"Provide a concrete example of the exact output layout you want","C":"Give up and reformat by hand every time","D":"Switch to a completely different task"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"When descriptions of a format keep missing, a worked example demonstrates the target directly. It is usually the durable fix that repeated re-describing is not.","explanationDistractor":"Reformatting by hand every time treats the symptom forever; an example fixes the root cause so Claude produces the right layout itself.","reference":null},{"id":"as-090","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Wrong Model","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A high-volume, simple text-cleanup job is running slowly and costing more than expected on your largest model. What is the root-cause fix?","options":{"A":"Add more instructions to the prompt","B":"Run it twice to be sure of the result","C":"Switch the job to a smaller, faster model suited to simple high-volume work","D":"Increase the maximum output length"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Running a simple, high-volume task on the largest model wastes cost and latency. Matching the model to the task, a smaller, faster model, fixes the root cause.","explanationDistractor":"Adding more instructions does not address the mismatch between a heavyweight model and a lightweight task; the model choice is the lever.","reference":null},{"id":"as-091","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Wrong Model","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"A genuinely complex, multi-step analysis produces shallow, error-prone results on a small, fast model. What is the most appropriate fix?","options":{"A":"Ask the question several more times on the same model","B":"Shorten the prompt so there is less to confuse it","C":"Lower the output length","D":"Move to a more capable model (and enable extended thinking) suited to hard reasoning"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"When a task genuinely exceeds a small model's reasoning, the fix is to match the task to a more capable model, and extended thinking, rather than retrying the same underpowered setup.","explanationDistractor":"Repeating the prompt on the same small model does not add the reasoning capacity the complex task needs; the model is the constraint.","reference":null},{"id":"as-092","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Durable Fixes","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Every day you correct Claude's tone the same way in a fresh chat. What is the durable fix?","options":{"A":"Put the tone rule in the Project's custom instructions once, so it applies automatically","B":"Keep correcting it by hand in every new chat, day after day","C":"Accept the off tone as an unavoidable limitation of the tool","D":"Try opening Claude in a different web browser instead"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"A repeated manual correction is a signal to fix the configuration. Putting the tone rule in the Project's custom instructions solves it once for every future chat.","explanationDistractor":"Correcting the tone by hand each day is the endlessly repeated one-off patch that a durable configuration change is meant to replace.","reference":null},{"id":"as-093","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Wrong Entry Point","scenario":"Codebase Edits","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A developer keeps pasting files from a large codebase into chat one at a time to get Claude to make coordinated edits, and it is painful and error-prone. What is the root-cause fix?","options":{"A":"Paste the files faster","B":"Use Claude Code, which works directly across the repository, instead of chat","C":"Paste all the files in a single giant message","D":"Reduce the number of files by deleting code"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"The pain comes from using the wrong entry point. Claude Code operates directly on the repository, eliminating the manual file-by-file pasting that chat forces.","explanationDistractor":"Pasting everything in one giant message still lacks the repository access and editing workflow that Claude Code provides, and it strains a single conversation.","reference":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview"},{"id":"as-094","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Stale Knowledge","scenario":null,"difficulty":"easy","stem":"Claude's answer about a fast-moving topic is confidently out of date. What is the most direct fix?","options":{"A":"Rephrase the question with synonyms","B":"Ask Claude to \"be more current\"","C":"Enable web search so Claude can retrieve up-to-date information","D":"Switch to a smaller model"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Out-of-date answers on recent topics stem from the training cutoff. Enabling web search lets Claude pull current information rather than relying on stale knowledge.","explanationDistractor":"Asking Claude to \"be more current\" cannot conjure post-cutoff facts it never learned; it needs a retrieval tool like web search.","reference":null},{"id":"as-095","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Ambiguous Prompt","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Two people give Claude \"clean up this text\" and get very different results, and neither is what they wanted. What is the root cause and fix?","options":{"A":"The model is just inconsistent, so nothing can really be done","B":"The text they gave it is simply far too long to handle","C":"They should each just ask the same thing several more times","D":"\"Clean up\" is ambiguous; specify what to change (grammar, tone, length, formatting)"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Ambiguous instructions like \"clean up\" invite divergent interpretations. Specifying exactly which changes you want removes the ambiguity and makes results predictable.","explanationDistractor":"The variability is not randomness to accept; it stems from an under-specified request, which precise instructions resolve.","reference":null},{"id":"as-096","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Isolating Variables","scenario":null,"difficulty":"hard","stem":"An output got worse after you edited your prompt, but you changed several things at once. What is the best troubleshooting move?","options":{"A":"Revert, then reintroduce the changes one at a time to find which caused the regression","B":"Change even more things at once and hope the result improves","C":"Abandon the prompt entirely and start over from nothing","D":"Assume the model itself degraded and just wait a day"},"answer":"A","explanationCorrect":"Isolating variables is core to troubleshooting. Reverting and reapplying changes one at a time reveals exactly which edit caused the regression, so you can fix it precisely.","explanationDistractor":"Changing even more at once makes the regression harder to diagnose, compounding the very problem that caused it.","reference":null},{"id":"as-097","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Right Diagnosis","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Claude gives wrong answers about your internal process. You are tempted to blame the model, but it was never given the process documentation. What is the real root cause?","options":{"A":"The model is fundamentally incapable of the task","B":"Missing context: Claude was not given the internal information it needs","C":"An incorrect temperature setting","D":"The time of day you asked"},"answer":"B","explanationCorrect":"Wrong answers about private information usually mean the context is missing, not that the model is incapable. Supplying the internal documentation is the fix.","explanationDistractor":"Blaming the model's core capability misdiagnoses the problem; Claude simply never received the internal information the answer depends on.","reference":null},{"id":"as-098","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Constraining Output","scenario":null,"difficulty":"medium","stem":"Claude's responses are far longer and more rambling than you need for a quick summary. What is the durable fix?","options":{"A":"Delete parts of each response by hand every time","B":"Ask it to \"be concise\" with no further detail and hope","C":"Specify explicit constraints, for example \"at most five bullet points, one line each\"","D":"Switch to a larger model"},"answer":"C","explanationCorrect":"Rambling output is best fixed by explicit constraints on length and structure. Concrete limits like \"five bullets, one line each\" reliably shape the response.","explanationDistractor":"Editing every response by hand is an endless one-off patch; setting a concrete length constraint fixes the behavior at the source.","reference":null},{"id":"as-099","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Root-Cause Thinking","scenario":null,"difficulty":"hard","stem":"A prompt underperforms. Which sequence best reflects sound troubleshooting?","options":{"A":"Immediately switch to the biggest available model and move on","B":"Randomly tweak the wording until something finally sticks","C":"Blame the model, decide it simply cannot do this, and stop using it","D":"Identify the likely root cause (ambiguous ask, missing context, wrong model or entry point) and fix it"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"Effective troubleshooting diagnoses the specific root cause, ambiguity, missing context, model fit, or entry point, before applying a targeted fix, rather than guessing.","explanationDistractor":"Randomly tweaking wording may occasionally help by luck but teaches you nothing and often misses the actual cause, like missing context or a model mismatch.","reference":null},{"id":"as-100","domain":"troubleshooting","studyArea":"Durable Fixes","scenario":"Team Standardization","difficulty":"medium","stem":"You discover a prompt tweak that reliably fixes a recurring problem across your team's chats. What is the best way to capture the win?","options":{"A":"Keep the tweak to yourself for next time","B":"Apply it manually whenever you happen to remember","C":"Mention it once in a meeting and move on","D":"Build it into the shared Project configuration so every chat benefits automatically"},"answer":"D","explanationCorrect":"A durable fix belongs in shared configuration. Encoding the improvement in the team's Project makes every future chat benefit automatically, instead of depending on individuals remembering to apply it.","explanationDistractor":"Applying the tweak manually whenever you remember is fragile and does not help teammates; building it into shared config makes the fix permanent and universal.","reference":null}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which entry point works directly across the files of a local code repository?","back":"Claude Code (the terminal tool) reads, edits, and runs code in your repo. Use claude.ai chat for quick Q&A, Projects for reusable context, and the API to build automation.","domain":"platform-models"},{"front":"When should you use a Claude Project instead of a plain chat?","back":"When you reuse the same instructions and reference material across many chats. A Project's custom instructions and knowledge sources apply to every conversation in it.","domain":"platform-models"},{"front":"When is the API the right entry point?","back":"When you need Claude embedded in your own software, running programmatically with no person in the loop.","domain":"platform-models"},{"front":"Which model for the hardest, multi-step reasoning?","back":"Claude Opus 4.8 — the most capable model. Reserve it for genuinely complex work where accuracy outweighs cost and speed.","domain":"platform-models"},{"front":"Which model for high-volume, balanced work?","back":"Claude Sonnet 5 — strong quality at a cost and speed that scale.","domain":"platform-models"},{"front":"Which model for simple, fast, high-volume tasks?","back":"Claude Haiku 4.5 — the lowest latency and cost for lightweight tasks like classification or language detection.","domain":"platform-models"},{"front":"What feature helps Claude reason through multi-step problems?","back":"Extended (adaptive) thinking — it lets Claude work through intermediate steps before answering.","domain":"platform-models"},{"front":"How do you get accurate answers about events after the training cutoff?","back":"Enable web search so Claude can retrieve current information; reasoning alone cannot recover facts it never learned.","domain":"platform-models"},{"front":"Your long chat is dragging in irrelevant earlier details. What is the fix?","back":"Start a fresh conversation focused on the current topic; everything earlier is context Claude may draw on.","domain":"platform-models"},{"front":"Why not run every task on the biggest model?","back":"Match the model to the task. Simple/high-volume work runs well on Haiku or Sonnet; Opus is for the hardest reasoning. Oversizing wastes cost and latency.","domain":"platform-models"},{"front":"What are the core elements of a well-structured prompt?","back":"Role, a clear task, relevant context, the desired output format, and examples.","domain":"prompting"},{"front":"Instructions keep missing a specific format. What works better?","back":"Show two or three worked input-to-output examples (few-shot). Demonstrating the pattern beats describing it.","domain":"prompting"},{"front":"Why change only one thing at a time when iterating?","back":"So you can tell which change caused the effect; changing many at once makes the result uninterpretable.","domain":"prompting"},{"front":"Best fix for a vague, off-target answer?","back":"Be specific — state the exact scope, format, and success criteria.","domain":"prompting"},{"front":"A complex request returns shallow results. What helps?","back":"Decompose it into focused steps; do and check each before it feeds the next.","domain":"prompting"},{"front":"'Don't be so formal' is not working. Better approach?","back":"Positively describe the tone you do want (warm, conversational) with a short example. Say what to do, not just what to avoid.","domain":"prompting"},{"front":"Claude does not know your internal policy. What is essential?","back":"Provide the policy text as context; capability cannot substitute for a document it never saw.","domain":"prompting"},{"front":"When are few-shot examples worth adding?","back":"When a task has a specific format or edge cases that plain instructions keep getting wrong. For simple tasks Claude already handles, skip them.","domain":"prompting"},{"front":"Does confident, fluent writing mean the answer is correct?","back":"No — Claude can be confidently and articulately wrong. Fluency is not a reliability signal.","domain":"evaluating"},{"front":"Claude cites a study or statistic. What must you do before publishing?","back":"Independently verify the claim and that the cited source actually says it; citations can be fabricated.","domain":"evaluating"},{"front":"A citation looks authoritative but you cannot find the source anywhere. Likely cause?","back":"A hallucination — a plausible-looking but nonexistent citation. Treat it as unverified.","domain":"evaluating"},{"front":"Which outputs require human verification?","back":"High-stakes, factual, legal/medical, or irreversible ones. Validate before you act or put your name on it.","domain":"evaluating"},{"front":"A real, working citation URL — is the claim now proven?","back":"No — confirm the linked source actually supports the specific claim, not just that the page exists.","domain":"evaluating"},{"front":"How much verification does an output need?","back":"Scale it with stakes and reversibility. Brainstorming needs little; a tax figure or contract needs independent human review.","domain":"evaluating"},{"front":"Claude's resume shortlist skews to one demographic. Response?","back":"Treat it as a possible bias signal — examine the criteria and keep human oversight.","domain":"evaluating"},{"front":"Who is accountable for Claude's output you send under your name?","back":"You are. Validate the facts and reasoning first.","domain":"evaluating"},{"front":"Which steps are best to delegate to Claude?","back":"Well-specified, repeatable, checkable, low-risk steps. Keep ambiguous, high-stakes, accountability-bearing judgment with a human.","domain":"workflow"},{"front":"What decides whether an action can be automated vs. needs human approval?","back":"The cost and reversibility of an error. Cheap and reversible can auto-proceed; costly or irreversible needs human review.","domain":"workflow"},{"front":"How do you describe a Claude workflow's value to stakeholders?","back":"Honestly — pair the real benefit with the limitations and where humans stay in the loop. Do not overpromise.","domain":"workflow"},{"front":"Moving from 'I use Claude' to 'our workflow uses Claude'?","back":"Capture working prompts, context, and configuration in a shared, reusable setup so the whole team gets consistent results.","domain":"workflow"},{"front":"Best first process to automate with Claude?","back":"A frequent, well-defined, low-risk task with clear inputs and checkable outputs.","domain":"workflow"},{"front":"Who should hold accountability for a final decision in an AI-assisted workflow?","back":"A designated human owner who reviews and approves. A tool cannot be accountable.","domain":"workflow"},{"front":"Your workflow only lives in your head. What is the risk?","back":"It is fragile. Document steps, prompts, and config so others can run it when you are away.","domain":"workflow"},{"front":"What do a Project's custom instructions do?","back":"They apply automatically to every chat in the Project — standing rules for tone, format, and behavior.","domain":"configuration"},{"front":"Where should reusable reference documents go in a Project?","back":"As knowledge sources. Tone and format rules go in custom instructions.","domain":"configuration"},{"front":"Claude keeps quoting last year's prices. Root-cause fix?","back":"Update the Project's knowledge source to current data. Stale config is a top failure cause.","domain":"configuration"},{"front":"Main advantage of Project context over per-prompt context?","back":"Every chat in the Project inherits it, raising quality and consistency across all of them.","domain":"configuration"},{"front":"Two unrelated kinds of work — one Project or two?","back":"Two. Separate Projects keep each set of instructions and knowledge focused and relevant.","domain":"configuration"},{"front":"How do you keep Project knowledge trustworthy over time?","back":"Review and refresh it on a regular cadence; Claude will not update your sources for you.","domain":"configuration"},{"front":"Conflicting policy versions in a Project's knowledge. Fix?","back":"Keep only the current authoritative version; remove outdated and draft copies so Claude cites the right one.","domain":"configuration"},{"front":"About to paste a config with a live API key. What do you do?","back":"Redact or remove the secret first. Never paste live credentials; asking Claude to 'ignore' it does not undo the exposure.","domain":"governance"},{"front":"Before using regulated personal data (PII/PHI) in a tool?","back":"Follow your data policy and get authorization; minimize or anonymize what you do not need.","domain":"governance"},{"front":"Most important thing to know before entering sensitive data?","back":"Where the data goes, how it may be used or retained, and whether policy permits it.","domain":"governance"},{"front":"Confidential doc plus an unapproved tool. Right move?","back":"Do not. Use only tools approved for that data class; deleting the chat later does not undo the exposure.","domain":"governance"},{"front":"What is data minimization?","back":"Include only the data the task requires, and no more, to reduce exposure of sensitive info.","domain":"governance"},{"front":"Why does data classification matter before pasting?","back":"It sets the handling rules — some classes may not be entered into certain tools at all.","domain":"governance"},{"front":"Employee uses a personal, unvetted AI account for company data. Concern?","back":"Company data flows outside approved controls and agreements — a core governance risk.","domain":"governance"},{"front":"Before sending Claude output to an external partner?","back":"Review it to ensure no confidential or personal internal data was carried into the output.","domain":"governance"},{"front":"Answers are generic and could fit any company. Root cause?","back":"A context-poor prompt. Add the specific details of your situation.","domain":"troubleshooting"},{"front":"You described the format three times and it is still wrong. Durable fix?","back":"Provide a concrete example of the exact output you want.","domain":"troubleshooting"},{"front":"Simple high-volume job is slow/costly on the biggest model. Fix?","back":"Switch to a smaller, faster model suited to the task.","domain":"troubleshooting"},{"front":"Same manual correction every day. Durable fix?","back":"Encode it once in the Project's custom instructions instead of patching each chat.","domain":"troubleshooting"},{"front":"Answer is confidently out of date on a recent topic. Fix?","back":"Enable web search — the gap is the training cutoff, not reasoning.","domain":"troubleshooting"},{"front":"An output regressed after you changed several things at once. Move?","back":"Revert and reintroduce changes one at a time to isolate the cause.","domain":"troubleshooting"},{"front":"What is a sound troubleshooting sequence?","back":"Diagnose the root cause — ambiguous ask, missing context, wrong model, or wrong entry point — then fix that specific cause durably.","domain":"troubleshooting"}]};
